1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:07,260
On a quiet stretch of the Rhine, we kept seeing them: small dimples on the water,

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,750
drifting beside the boat.

3
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:13,140
Some lasted a surprisingly long time.

4
00:00:13,690 --> 00:00:16,950
Each one is the top of a whirlpool.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:26,180
Spinning water needs an inward push, and pressure provides it: higher outside, lower toward the centre.

6
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:30,340
So above the low-pressure core, the surface dips.

7
00:00:30,890 --> 00:00:33,870
But a river is restless.

8
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:37,550
Everything in it gets sheared and stirred.

9
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:41,100
So what keeps one tiny whirlpool going?

10
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:49,470
Dive under one, and you find a narrow tube of spinning water, reaching down into the river.

11
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:57,120
Draw a line along its middle, and you have a vortex line.

12
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:02,580
The water turns around it; the line is just how we draw the spin.

13
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:06,940
A line meets the surface in a single point.

14
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,080
That point is the dimple.

15
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:16,240
In 1858, Hermann von Helmholtz found a strange rule:

16
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:21,510
a tube of spin can't just have a loose end inside the water.

17
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:25,990
To see why, measure the spin with a loop.

18
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:32,290
Go around it, adding up how fast the water flows along each little piece.

19
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:35,230
That total is the circulation.

20
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:41,700
Now freeze the flow, and slide the loop along the tube and past its end,

21
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:45,140
never crossing any spinning water.

22
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:48,260
The circulation can't change.

23
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:54,870
But past the end, the loop can shrink to nothing, and nothing has no circulation.

24
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,811
So the tube can't just stop.

25
00:01:58,511 --> 00:02:05,551
It has to close into a ring, or end on something: the riverbed, or the surface.

26
00:02:06,901 --> 00:02:13,761
A smoke ring is that ring: a tube of spinning air, closed on itself.

27
00:02:13,701 --> 00:02:16,401
The smoke lets you see it roll.

28
00:02:18,751 --> 00:02:27,571
In 1867, in Edinburgh, Peter Guthrie Tait showed his smoke rings to his friend William Thomson,

29
00:02:27,511 --> 00:02:30,061
later Lord Kelvin.

30
00:02:30,611 --> 00:02:35,021
The rings bounced and wobbled without falling apart.

31
00:02:34,961 --> 00:02:40,301
Two rings travelling together can even take turns passing through each other.

32
00:02:43,351 --> 00:02:47,681
Kelvin saw an answer to a much bigger question.

33
00:02:47,621 --> 00:02:53,921
In a perfect, frictionless fluid, a ring's circulation would never fade.

34
00:02:53,861 --> 00:02:59,891
So maybe atoms were vortex rings in the ether, and each element a different knot.

35
00:03:01,941 --> 00:03:04,941
Kelvin was wrong about atoms.

36
00:03:04,881 --> 00:03:09,991
But Tait's attempts to catalogue the knots helped start knot theory.

37
00:03:12,341 --> 00:03:16,131
Now bring a ring up under the water's surface.

38
00:03:16,071 --> 00:03:21,801
Tilted, its upper edge arrives first, and it can reconnect to the surface,

39
00:03:21,741 --> 00:03:24,881
leaving a curved tube with two ends.

40
00:03:25,431 --> 00:03:28,621
From above, two dimples.

41
00:03:28,561 --> 00:03:31,811
From below, one connected tube.

42
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:40,731
Its spin points up at one end and down at the other, so from above,

43
00:03:40,671 --> 00:03:43,961
the two dimples turn opposite ways.

44
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:48,961
The Rhine doesn't need a smoke box.

45
00:03:48,901 --> 00:03:52,431
The current makes spinning structures for itself.

46
00:03:52,981 --> 00:03:55,891
One source is the riverbed.

47
00:03:55,831 --> 00:04:01,911
Friction slows the water nearest the bottom, while the water above races past,

48
00:04:01,851 --> 00:04:06,891
and that shear rolls up into arches, like half smoke rings.

49
00:04:07,441 --> 00:04:13,181
It happens all the time: the riverbed is a vortex factory.

50
00:04:13,121 --> 00:04:17,701
Most arches are torn apart long before they reach the top.

51
00:04:18,251 --> 00:04:25,301
But some rise and connect to the surface, leaving a pair of dimples, turning opposite ways.

52
00:04:25,851 --> 00:04:30,761
Each rides the other's swirl, carrying the pair along.

53
00:04:30,701 --> 00:04:34,271
Bring them closer, and they go faster.

54
00:04:35,621 --> 00:04:38,621
So what keeps a whirlpool going?

55
00:04:39,171 --> 00:04:44,761
Left alone, friction spreads its spin into the water around it.

56
00:04:44,701 --> 00:04:48,621
The core widens, and the dimple gets shallower.

57
00:04:49,171 --> 00:04:52,971
But the river can also work the other way.

58
00:04:52,911 --> 00:05:01,131
Where surface water converges and sinks, the downward flow stretches an upright whirlpool and spins it faster.

59
00:05:01,681 --> 00:05:05,981
The surface can look perfectly flat while this happens.

60
00:05:05,921 --> 00:05:11,591
Flat-looking water isn't the same as water moving only sideways.

61
00:05:12,141 --> 00:05:16,711
So turbulence doesn't only tear whirlpools down.

62
00:05:16,651 --> 00:05:19,201
It can also keep them going.

63
00:05:20,951 --> 00:05:26,041
And a dimple can vanish from view before its whirlpool does.

64
00:05:25,981 --> 00:05:31,061
Ripples can hide a dimple without destroying the whirlpool underneath.

65
00:05:32,411 --> 00:05:38,181
Those hidden motions are exactly what researchers are learning to read from the surface.

66
00:05:38,731 --> 00:05:45,591
A team in Trondheim, Norway, including Omer Babiker and Simen Ellingsen,

67
00:05:45,531 --> 00:05:52,821
found in simulations that the number of dimples tracked how strongly the surface was spreading and converging.

68
00:05:53,371 --> 00:05:57,801
This year, laboratory experiments backed up the link,

69
00:05:57,741 --> 00:06:04,801
this time measuring the area covered by dimples and by longer marks called scars.

70
00:06:05,351 --> 00:06:08,331
That churning renews the surface,

71
00:06:08,271 --> 00:06:15,651
and it helps set how fast gases like oxygen and carbon dioxide cross between water and air.

72
00:06:16,201 --> 00:06:20,521
A dimple isn't a map of everything underneath.

73
00:06:20,461 --> 00:06:25,261
But watch one long enough, and you're reading a little of the river below.
